WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 91: The Great Johnstown Flood of 1889: The South Fork Dam Disaster

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:17.360
[Rebecca] Today, we step back into the gilded age of late 19th century Pennsylvania to witness one of the most catastrophic engineering disasters and human tragedies in American history.

4
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.600
[Rebecca] The great Johnstown flood of May 31st, 1889.

5
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:30.480
[Rebecca] High in the Allegheny Mountains, a prestigious summer resort for Pittsburgh's elite industrialists, sat perched above a colossal earthen dam.

6
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:45.440
[Rebecca] When unprecedented rainfall overwhelmed the compromised structure, 20 million tons of water roared down the mountain at 40 miles per hour, scouring entire communities from the map and carrying a blazing mountain of wreckage into the heart of Johnstown.

7
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:52.960
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full, heart-pounding account of that fateful afternoon.

8
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:15.920
[George] Situated deep in a narrow valley in the Allegheny Mountains, at the confluence of the Stana Creek and Little Canemo Rivers, the city was home to 30,000 residents, predominantly German, Irish, Welsh, and Eastern European immigrants.

9
00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:28.320
[George] At its beating industrial heart stood the Gargantuan Cambria Iron Company, which pioneered the mass production of high-tensile steel rails that stitched together the transcontinental railroads of the United States.

10
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:37.520
[George] Cambria was one of the largest and most technologically advanced steel mills in the world, its blast furnaces illuminating the mountain skies night and day.

11
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.520
[George] Yet Johnstone's geography was as precarious as it was productive.

12
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:52.960
[George] Hemmed in by steep mountain walls rising hundreds of feet on all sides, the city occupied a natural floodplain where spring thaws and heavy rains routinely pushed the rivers over their banks.

13
00:01:52.960 --> 00:02:03.480
[George] Longtime residents were accustomed to ankle-deep or knee-deep water filling their cellars every few years, treating seasonal freshets as an ordinary inconvenience of valley life.

14
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:10.440
[George] Furniture was moved to second floors, carpets were rolled up, and commerce continued largely uninterrupted.

15
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:19.080
[George] But 14 miles upriver and 450 feet higher in elevation lay an artificial leviathan that turned their valley into a lethal funnel.

16
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:28.200
[George] High on the mountain near South Fork stood Lake Canema, a colossal two-mile-long artificial reservoir holding over 20 million tons of water.

17
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:40.280
[George] Originally built by the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania in the 1850s to supply water for the main line canal system, the Earthen South Fork Dam had been abandoned when canals were eclipsed by railroads.

18
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:58.440
[George] In 1879, the neglected reservoir was purchased by a private syndicate of wealthy Pittsburgh steel and banking tycoons, including Andrew Carnegie, Henry Clay Frick, and Andrew Mellon, who transformed it into an exclusive mountain retreat, the South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club.

19
00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:12.120
[George] To create their idyllic summer paradise, the members of the South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club undertook extensive modifications to the historic earthen dam, modifications executed with breathtaking engineering carelessness.

20
00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:22.120
[George] Built of compacted earth, clay and gravel rather than reinforced masonry, the dam was over 70 feet high and 900 feet long across its crest.

21
00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:37.640
[George] To make the top of the dam wide enough to drive two carriages abreast, club managers lowered the center of the earthen crest by several feet, dangerously reducing the structure's freeboard, the critical margin of safety between the lake's surface and the top of the dam.

22
00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:49.040
[George] Worse still, to prevent valuable black bass and pickerels stocked in the lake from escaping downstream into the river, the club installed heavy wire mesh fish screens across the stone spillway.

23
00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:59.760
[George] These screens acted as a filter that quickly trapped floating logs, dead branches, and forest debris during storms, effectively choking the spillway's discharge capacity.

24
00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:15.840
[George] Furthermore, the original iron discharge pipes at the base of the dam, designed to drain the lake in an emergency, had been removed and sold for scrap years earlier by prior owners, leaving the club with no mechanism to lower water levels during heavy rainfall.

25
00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:31.280
[George] Experienced hydraulic engineers from the Cambria Iron Company had inspected the dam and warned the club repeatedly that an extreme storm could cause water to overtop the lowered earthen crest, which would rapidly erode the clay core and trigger a catastrophic breach.

26
00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:41.760
[George] But club administrators dismissed these warnings as alarmist rhetoric, assuring local authorities that the massive earthen wall had held for decades and remained completely secure.

27
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:54.000
[George] On the night of Thursday, May 30, 1889, a ferocious tropical storm tracking northward from the Gulf of Mexico collided with cold northern air directly over western Pennsylvania.

28
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.120
[George] The heavens opened, unleashing the heaviest rainfall ever recorded in the region.

29
00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:06.720
[George] Between six and ten inches of water fell within 24 hours, transforming every mountain creek into a raging torrent.

30
00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:21.120
[George] By dawn on Friday, May 31, Johnstown was already submerged in two to eight feet of muddy water from the overflowing Stana Creek and Little Canemo rivers, with residents moving to their upper stories as they had done many times before.

31
00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.320
[George] Fourteen miles up the mountain at the South Fork Dam, club's superintendent John G.

32
00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:27.360
[George] Park J.R.

33
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.520
[George] awoke to find Lake Canemo rising at an alarming rate of nearly a foot per hour.

34
00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:42.440
[George] Debris from the surrounding mountainsides was washing into the lake, completely clogging the fish screens at the spillway and causing water to back up against the earthen wall.

35
00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:50.520
[George] Park mobilized a crew of twenty laborers to try to clear the mesh with pitchforks, but the force of the surging water made it impossible.

36
00:05:50.520 --> 00:06:01.240
[George] In desperation, Park ordered men to dig a secondary trench around the hillside and attempted to plough a furrow to relieve pressure, but the rocky ground resisted their picks.

37
00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:13.000
[George] By noon, water began to spill over the center of the earthen crest, running down the exterior face of the dam in small waterfalls that began eating away the clay and shale embankment like sugar.

38
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:22.360
[George] Recognizing that total collapse was imminent, Park sent a rider on horseback to the telegraph station in South Fork to flash urgent warnings down the valley.

39
00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.760
[George] The dam was becoming dangerous, and people should flee to high ground.

40
00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:37.880
[George] But because similar false alarms had been circulated during heavy rains in previous years, many valley residents and telegraph operators dismissed the messages as exaggerated panics.

41
00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:46.040
[George] At 3:10 on Friday afternoon, the center of the South Fork Dam gave way with a thunderous roar heard miles down the valley.

42
00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:57.240
[George] A 400-foot breach opened in the earthen wall, and twenty million tons of water cascaded outward in a colossal cataract, emptying Lake Kanemo in less than 45 minutes.

43
00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:01.520
[George] The surging mass, containing over four billion gallons of water.

44
00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:15.840
[George] Plunged into the narrow little Kanemo Gorge, carving away the mountainsides and picking up thousands of mature hemlock and oak trees, boulders weighing dozens of tons, and millions of board feet of commercial lumber from sawmills along the banks.

45
00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:21.520
[George] The flood wave moved down the winding valley like a solid battering ram of liquid doom.

46
00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:35.520
[George] It annihilated the village of South Fork, demolished the town of Mineral Point, and struck the town of East Kenema, where dozens of massive 60-ton steam locomotives were tossed like toy models and rolled hundreds of feet downstream.

47
00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.360
[George] Engineer John Hess.

48
00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:53.440
[George] Spotting the looming mountain of brown water and debris roaring down the tracks, tied down his locomotive whistle, sounding a desperate, continuous scream that alerted hundreds of workers and families to sprint for the valley walls seconds before the deluge pulverized his train.

49
00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:58.160
[George] By the time the wave approached Johnstown, it was no longer just water.

50
00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:10.080
[George] It was a churning, grinding mountain of devastation forty feet high, moving at forty miles per hour, pushed ahead by a violent blast of hurricane force wind created by the displaced air.

51
00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:24.160
[George] The black mass contained over 1600 demolished buildings, thousands of miles of tangled telegraph wire, steel rails, livestock and trees, turning the entire mixture into an unstoppable planetary plow.

52
00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:30.400
[George] At 4.07 in the afternoon, the wall of death struck Johnstown with apocalyptic ferocity.

53
00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:38.560
[George] The impact was so colossal that buildings were not merely flooded, they were instantly crushed into splinters as if struck by heavy artillery.

54
00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:50.480
[George] Brick churches, steel-framed warehouses, and entire rows of wooden homes were lifted intact off their foundations, hurled into adjacent structures, and ground into kindling within seconds.

55
00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:55.520
[George] Thousands of people trapped on the second floors of their homes had no opportunity to run.

56
00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:59.520
[George] Entire families vanished together beneath the churning wall of wreckage.

57
00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:10.680
[George] Downstream from Johnstown stood the monumental Stone Bridge, an imposing seven-arched stone masonry viaduct carrying the Pennsylvania Railroad mainline across the Canemo River.

58
00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:21.400
[George] The massive stone piers withstood the tremendous impact of the water, but the openings beneath the arches were quickly clogged by crushed houses, thousands of trees and debris.

59
00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:36.280
[George] Within minutes, a gargantuan 45-acre debris dam, towering forty feet above the river, jammed against the bridge, trapping over four thousand terrified men, women, and children who were clinging to overturned roofs and tangled timber.

60
00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:38.440
[George] Then came the ultimate horror.

61
00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:44.920
[George] Oil spilled from shattered tank cars and overturned cooking stoves ignited the compressed mountain of wreckage.

62
00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:54.360
[George] Fueled by dry lumber and howling winds, a terrifying fire broke out across the face of the debris pile, turning the river into a roaring crematorium.

63
00:09:54.360 --> 00:10:09.640
[George] For hours into the rainy night, hundreds of helpless people who had survived the violent flood waters were slowly burned alive in full view of loved ones trapped on the surrounding hillsides, their cries echoing through the dark valley until the flames finally died at dawn.

64
00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:18.120
[George] When the waters receded on Saturday morning, Johnstown was an unmitigated wasteland of mud, corpses, and smoldering ash.

65
00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:20.680
[George] The human toll was catastrophic.

66
00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:28.680
[George] 2,209 people had lost their lives, including 99 entire families wiped out without a single survivor.

67
00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.960
[George] Nearly 400 of the victims were small children.

68
00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] More than 700 bodies were so disfigured by the churning debris and fire that they could never be identified, ultimately laid to rest in a vast common grave at Grandview Cemetery beneath an ocean of blank white marble headstones.

69
00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:58.640
[George] The disaster prompted the first major nationwide relief effort in American history, generating over $3 million in private charitable donations from across the United States and 13 foreign nations.

70
00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:05.600
[George] Leading the humanitarian response was 67-year-old Clara Barton, founder of the American Red Cross.

71
00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:13.680
[George] Arriving on June 5, Barton established Johnstown as the fledgling organization's first major peacetime disaster operation.

72
00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:29.680
[George] For five grueling months, Barton and her volunteers lived in tents amidst typhoid outbreaks, coordinating field hospitals, distributing food, and constructing prefabricated temporary wooden barracks that sheltered over 25,000 homeless survivors.

73
00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:38.640
[George] The triumphant relief campaign in Johnstown solidified the American Red Cross's reputation as a vital national humanitarian institution.

74
00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:54.000
[George] Meanwhile, morticians and recovery workers spent months clearing thousands of tons of wreckage, uncovering buried victims along riverbanks as far downstream as Pittsburgh, 40 miles away, and continuing to find human remains for over two decades.

75
00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:03.680
[George] In the wake of the tragedy, public outrage across the United States burned with incandescent fury against the wealthy members of the South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club.

76
00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:18.160
[George] Newspapers from New York to San Francisco condemned Carnegie, Frick, and their associates for reckless corporate negligence, pointing out that their pursuit of personal pleasure had created the instrument of death that exterminated a working class city.

77
00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:22.720
[George] Yet in the courts of law, the victims of Johnstown were dealt a bitter defeat.

78
00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:34.600
[George] Under the prevailing legal doctrines of 19th century America, plaintiffs bore the impossible burden of proving specific criminal intent or direct individual negligence against club trustees.

79
00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:48.360
[George] The club's formidable legal defense team argued successfully that the unprecedented rainstorm was an unpredictable act of God that would have destroyed any structure, absolving the wealthy members of any civil or criminal liability.

80
00:12:48.360 --> 00:13:02.120
[George] Not a single cent in legal damages was ever awarded to the flood survivors from the South Fork Club or its members, though several tycoons, including Carnegie, made substantial private charitable donations toward rebuilding.

81
00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:16.120
[George] The profound injustice of Johnstown catalyzed a fundamental revolution in American tort law, shifting jurisprudence toward the modern doctrine of strict liability for hazardous activities and dangerous artificial reservoirs.

82
00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:28.600
[George] Furthermore, it led to the enactment of the nation's first rigorous state and federal dam safety inspection laws, ensuring that private dams would never again be altered without independent engineering oversight.

83
00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:42.760
[George] Today, Johnstown stands proud and resilient, but the stone abutments of the broken dam at South Fork remain preserved as a solemn national memorial, reminding the world of the terrible human cost of corporate carelessness.

84
00:13:52.920 --> 00:14:01.320
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we visit Menlo Park, New Jersey for the story of Thomas Edison's world changing invention factory.

